  "query_preview": "/THURSDAY NIGHT THROUGH WEDNESDAY/...\nIssued at 254 PM CDT Wed Aug 5 2026\n\nConfidence is greater in showers and thunderstorms forming Thursday \nnight as another LLJ develops but the location is not completely \nclear, although conceptually to the south and east of tonight's \nround would be most favored (i.e., southeastern MO/southwestern IL). \nAlthough there could be some training of thunderstorms, the overall \npattern does not look as favorable for heavy rainfall/flash flooding \nwith lower PW, weaker LLJ, and weaker boundary/front.\n\nYet another round of showers and thunderstorms is expected sometime \nbetween Friday afternoon and early Saturday morning as an upper-\nlevel shortwave trough or MCV propagates through the Mid-Mississippi \nRiver Valley and then another LLJ develops. If this round impacts \nareas that receive heavy rainfall tonight/Thursday morning, there \nwould be at least a low threat of flash flooding, but the pattern \nonce again does not appear as optimal for flash flooding overall.\n\nSaturday through Monday, global model guidance indicate that gradual \nheight rises associated with an upper-level ridge impinging on the \nMid-Mississippi River Valley from the west will help heat and \nhumidity build into the CWA. With high temperatures warming into the \n90s F across most of the area by Sunday and dewpoints also rising \ninto the 70s F, multiple days of peak heat index values reaching \naround 100 to 105 F are possible. However, with time, there is some \nconcern that a potential weakening of the ridge and longwave \ntroughing across the Great Lakes could facilitate southward \nadvancement of a front near or into the CWA along with accompanying \nshowers, thunderstorms, and cloud cover.",
